      "writtenOn": "2016-12-29T21:46:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The nvmem library seems like it\u0027s generic and wants to ensure nvmem_commit() pushes the update. Now that we\u0027re deferring this aren\u0027t we breaking the semantics for the nvmem expectations? Also, it\u0027s not clear to me if we should be delaying *all* nvmem commits. Is there functionality where we don\u0027t want that delay?",
      "revId": "c0a80397f55222e25c4ec5d8d7e97f9c6788a5d2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b75ab266_b086ecfd",
        "filename": "common/nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 519,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-12-29T21:57:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "the nvmem library works as promised unless nvmem_disable_commits() is invoked. And it is invoked by the TPM task, which is fully aware of the implications.\n\nI don\u0027t see availability of this capability as a problem.\n\nI don\u0027t believe there is anything during startup which would cause problems if it were not committed before a reset. \n\nMoreover, If there is no H1 reboot - the cache will be committed on TPM reset, so there is no difference whatsoever.\n\nIf there is an H1 reboot - the TPM would start from the NVRAM contents during the earlier start, worse comes to worst rollback counters might have to be incremented again.\n\nIn any case I suggest we discuss the concerns on the bug.",
      "parentUuid": "b75ab266_05ddd82a",
      "revId": "c0a80397f55222e25c4ec5d8d7e97f9c6788a5d2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b75ab266_10e358bf",
        "filename": "common/nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 519,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-12-29T22:02:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My point is that this is global. If nvmem is global as well, then the tpm task is just asserting control for all users. I\u0027m not sure if that\u0027s expected, but I could imagine people being confused by some other task not allowing their commits to go through.",
      "parentUuid": "b75ab266_b086ecfd",
      "revId": "c0a80397f55222e25c4ec5d8d7e97f9c6788a5d2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
